items.text:
She left the rose upon the sill;
For Lilia’s sake she chose the thorn.
The garden wept a bitter chill;
Her heart was cold before she mourn.

He saw the choice with eyes of fire;
And turned his face away from her.
The winter came with endless ire;
To freeze the love that was so dear.

O Earth, preserve the silence she hath made;
Beneath thy rain her weary heart is fed;
No dream shall vex the pillow of her head;
Nor shall the dawn rebuke thy quiet shade.
Her little portion in thy palm is weighed,
And every unavailing word is said;
She shall not labour for her daily bread;
Her hands lie open, all their service paid.
Receive her, Earth: the seed without a name
Who brings no bargain, neither gold nor tears;
Thy rains pass over, and thy roots go deep,
No wind shall fan her sorrow into flame.
God holds her days within a pinch of dust
Until His morning shall unseal her sleep.

The steel is cold against his palm;
He grips the hilt with trembling hand.
His eyes are bright with childish awe;
He asks what name this iron bears.

The sergeant speaks of duty’s weight;
The boy sees only silver shine.
His fingers close on smooth, hard wood;
He feels the grain beneath his touch.

The muzzle points toward the sky;
He smiles at shadows on the wall.
His youth remains unbroken here;
The gun is just a thing of brass.

O Earth, lie heavily upon her eyes;
Seal her sweet eyes weary of watching, Earth;
Lie close around her; leave no room for mirth
With its harsh laughter, nor for sound of sighs.
She hath no questions, she hath no replies,
Hushed in and curtained with a blessed dearth
Of all that irked her from the hour of birth;
With stillness that is almost Paradise.
Darkness more clear than noonday holdeth her,
Silence more musical than any song;
Even her very heart has ceased to stir:
Until the morning of Eternity
Her rest shall not begin nor end, but be;
And when she wakes she will not think it long.

Cold earth, thou art the keeper of her sleep;
The grass grows soft above her silent head;
No more the wind shall make her weary keep
The watch against the winter’s bitter spread.
I lay my hand upon the turf and feel
The weight of years that time hath set to rest;
She is no longer bound by mortal steel
To do the tasks which duty deems the best.
O grave, receive her into thy deep fold;
The sun shall shine upon the mossy ground;
Her spirit walks where no shadow is told,
And leaves this flesh to peace and safety found;
For in the dark she finds the light of God;
Her rest is sweet, and her burden gone.

O earth, be gentle with her where she lies;
Lay soft thy turf upon her folded hands;
She asks no more of thee than quiet lands
And dew at morning under paler skies.
She wearied long of tears and heavy sighs;
She loosed her hold on all the shifting sands;
Now bind her round, with thy green swaddling bands
And hush her till the trumpet bid her rise.
She is not thine for ever: thou dost keep
A seed in trust against the harvest morn;
Then hold her close, and fold her warm and deep
And let no wind disturb her, and no thorn;
For she who toiled so long is gone to sleep,
And sleeping, shall not weep, nor be forlorn.

Receive her, Earth, within thy quiet deep;
And give her weary heart a perfect rest;
No trouble can approach her folded breast
Nor heavy dreams disturb her settled sleep:
She has no tears, nor any cause to weep;
Be kind to her, an uncomplaining guest,
Who turns away from all the fading west;
And in thy silence thou the watch wilt keep;
Above her let the willow cast its shade,
Where she hath now forgotten all her pain
And heeds no longer any wind or rain;
Without a line to carve upon the stone
Beneath the sod where she at last is laid,
To lie with thee content and all alone.
